  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals a deep trough digging into \nthe Plains/Midwest with several embedded shortwaves and a plume of \nmoisture stretching from the Arklatex north into the UP. Analyzed \nPWATs reveals an area of ~1.5\" overhead the UP, which is the actual \nmaximum of NAEFS PWAT climo for this time of year. Isentropic ascent \noccuring along side this impressive moisture return is forcing \nwidespread stratiform rain this afternoon with a few embedded \nstronger showers within. Additionally, areas of fog are noted on \nregional observations. At the surface, a broad area of ~1000-1004 mb \nlow pressure stretches from northern Lake Superior down to northern \nMissouri, with the lower end found in northern Missouri under the \ncore of an upper level jet. This trough and surface low close off \nand become more stacked as it slowly lifts north overhead the UP by \ntomorrow morning, sending additional waves of much needed rainfall \novernight. Precipitation becomes more scattered and isolated as the \nstacked low becomes placed over western Lake Superior and the dry \nslot is placed over east-central Upper Michigan by Wednesday \nafternoon. Late Wednesday through Thursday, the stacked low \ntransitions to an open wave as its swept up in troughing descending \nacross northern Ontario. This works to lift the surface low north \nand east of the UP, allowing deformation driven and lake enhanced \nprecipitation to occur Thursday through early Friday. Strong \npressure rises and modest cold air advection aloft will drive NNE \ngales 35-45 kts over the Lake Superior on Thursday. Wouldn't be \nsurprised to see a transition to snow late Thursday for areas of the \ninterior, though no accumulations are expected. All in all, total \nprecipitation amounts today through Thursday will range from 0.5\" \n(mainly south and east) to upwards of 2\" in the west and Keweenaw. \nHopefully this help mitigate ongoing drought concerns, especially \ngiven warmer and drier than normal conditions return into the weekend \nand potentially further into the middle of November, per CPC \noutlooks.",
